+# Daily Grind security audit
+
+This is a static page with no application JavaScript, forms, storage, cookies,
+API calls, or bundled dependencies. The original page contained a style block,
+one style attribute, and an HTTPS Unsplash background image. No inline event
+handlers, executable URLs, DOM injection sinks, or third-party scripts were found.
+There was no response-header configuration in the repository. Live response
+headers and backend routes were not inspected.
+
+## Implemented controls
+
+- Extracted the existing CSS into `styles.css` and replaced the inline heading
+  style with a class, preserving the design without permitting inline styles.
+- Added an early CSP meta fallback. The policy denies scripts (including event
+  handlers), plugins, base URL changes, forms, frames, connections, and other
+  resource types by default. It permits local styles and images from the existing
+  `https://images.unsplash.com` origin. Navigation links still work; CSP resource
+  directives do not restrict ordinary top-level navigation.
+- Restricted Trusted Types sinks and policy creation in supporting browsers.
+  There is no permissive default policy or runtime code that requires exceptions.
+- Added a no-referrer meta fallback so the external image receives no referrer.
+- Added equivalent `_headers` and `vercel.json` response headers: full CSP with
+  `frame-ancestors 'none'`, legacy framing denial, MIME sniffing prevention,
+  no-referrer, and disabled unused device/payment permissions.
+
+## Deployment and policy changes
+
+Serve the root static assets over HTTPS. On Cloudflare Pages or Netlify, include
+`_headers` in the published directory. Vercel reads `vercel.json` at the project
+root. Other hosts must translate those headers into their response configuration.
+The existing `start` command is not proof that these host-specific configurations
+are applied. No server was started or server entrypoint imported during this work.
+
+The HTML fallback works independently of hosting configuration, but cannot provide
+framing protection, `nosniff`, or Permissions Policy. These require HTTP response
+headers. Apply headers to error pages and future backend routes as well. Serve
+`styles.css` as `text/css`. HSTS is intentionally left to the deployment owner:
+enable it after HTTPS is confirmed; include subdomains or preload only after
+checking those domains too.
+
+The current restrictive policy can be enforced because the page has no scripts
+or remaining inline styles. For future policy changes, keep this baseline and
+trial a candidate in the **HTTP** `Content-Security-Policy-Report-Only` header.
+Report-only CSP cannot be delivered through a meta tag. Reports require a real,
+operated collector configured using `report-to`/`Reporting-Endpoints` (and
+`report-uri` if legacy reporting is needed); no nonfunctional collector URL is
+configured here. Avoid logging full URLs, query strings, or script samples that
+may contain personal data. Review violations and intended resource loads before
+promoting the candidate, then update both host configurations and the meta fallback.
+An existing enforcing policy still blocks resources during a report-only trial;
+new features must account for that baseline before release.
+
+The `/logout` GET link and account/order destinations have no implementations in
+this repository. If logout changes session state, implement it server-side using
+a CSRF-protected POST and update the markup and `form-action` policy together.
+Session cookies, authentication, CSRF defenses, and backend input validation
+cannot be implemented or audited from this static page.
+
+## Validation
+
+Run `npm test` for dependency-free static regression checks covering policy
+parity, required restrictions, resource compatibility, forbidden inline execution,
+and header configuration parity. These checks do not demonstrate that a live CDN
+or web server emits the configured headers. No browser verification was performed.
+
+References: [MDN CSP documentation](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTTP/Reference/Headers/Content-Security-Policy),
+[Cloudflare static headers](https://developers.cloudflare.com/pages/configuration/headers/),
+and [Vercel project configuration](https://vercel.com/docs/project-configuration).
